{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Large-data charting starts with representation, not renderer throughput."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ask:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What question must the chart answer?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Must a reader inspect every observation?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How many independently useful marks fit in the available pixels?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which totals, extrema, order, or identities must never be lost?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How frequently does the source update?"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Raw output is correct only while independently rendering every observation is\nboth meaningful and affordable."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"count-every-stage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Count every stage"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Track four quantities:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"source"}]},{"type":"text","value":": rows received by the application;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"represented"}]},{"type":"text","value":": source rows accounted for by the encoding;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"prepared"}]},{"type":"text","value":": rows or vertices passed to marks;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"rendered"}]},{"type":"text","value":": scene nodes and path vertices, plus the selected renderer's DOM\nor draw work."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not describe a million-row chart as a million rendered points when the\nvisible result is a few thousand aggregate cells. The bounded output is a\nfeature, but its accounting must be honest."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"bounded-representations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bounded representations"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Problem"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Representation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Invariant"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dense point cloud"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed density grid"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell counts sum to source count"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"High-rate ordered signal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pixel-width first/min/max/last envelope"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every row belongs to one bucket and global extrema survive"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric distribution"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed-boundary histogram"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bin counts sum to source count"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"High-cardinality categories"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leading categories plus Other"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leading and remainder totals sum to source count"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Large time range with a viewport"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Domain-clipped or width-bounded envelope"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Visible extrema and boundary continuity survive"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose bucket count from the task and available resolution. A test fixture's\ngrid or threshold count is not a universal default."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"73-many-point-scatter","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-raw-output-is-still-useful","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When raw output is still useful"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One line path can retain many ordered observations without creating one DOM\nnode per row."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A scatterplot creates an independently addressable mark for every\nobservation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable keys make rolling updates reuse surviving nodes."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Identity reuse reduces allocation and replacement. It cannot make ten thousand\nindependent SVG elements a sensible default when those elements compete for\nthe same pixels."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Switch representations before the raw update cost exceeds the product's frame\nbudget or the output stops being readable."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-canvas-helps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When Canvas helps"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The optional Canvas renderer removes per-node SVG DOM and reconciliation cost.\nIt can be useful when a measured bottleneck is SVG element creation or paint.\nIt does not remove channel materialization, scale and guide work, scene\ncompilation, path construction, or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartPoint"},{"type":"text","value":" creation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A dot mark still produces one scene node and one interaction point per\nobservation. Default nearest-point focus still scans those points linearly.\nSupply a measured "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spatialIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" when individual points are still the correct\npointer target, and use a focus strategy with a bounded "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"navigation"},{"type":"text","value":" order\nwhen every observation is not a useful keyboard stop."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not treat Canvas as permission to promise a million independently\ninteractive marks. Compare source, represented, prepared, and rendered counts;\nmeasure compilation, paint, interaction, and memory; then aggregate, sample,\nor bound the window when the representation exceeds the product budget."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"transform-ownership","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transform ownership"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Put width-independent aggregation in application code. Memoize it with the\nframework's computed-state primitive when it should survive a presentation\nchange."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Surface-responsive transforms may use the responsive builder's full scene width.\nExact screen-space transforms need the final inner plot bounds and resolved\nscales, so implement them in a custom mark's render phase or an\napplication-owned overlay. Keep either cost visible rather than hiding it in\nan unrelated renderer."}]},{"type":"code","value":"function summarizeDensity(rows: Input['rows']) {\n  return summarizeSource(rows)\n}\n\nfunction createDensityChart(rows: Input['rows']) {\n  const summary = summarizeDensity(rows)\n\n  return defineChart(({ width }) => {\n    const thresholds = width < 480 ? 12 : width < 900 ? 24 : 40\n    const bins = buildSemanticBins(summary, { thresholds })\n    return densitySpec(bins)\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The full scene dimensions equal final plot dimensions only for a deliberately\nguide-free, zero-margin chart. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./responsive-charts.md#responsive-construction","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responsive Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the\ngeometry boundary."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the official D3 modules linked from\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for grouping, binning, summary\nstatistics, spatial indexing, and shape preparation. TanStack Charts consumes\nthe resulting rows and intervals."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"interaction-over-dense-output","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interaction over dense output"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"First decide whether the encoded mark or the original observation is the focus\ntarget."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus aggregate cells when the chart communicates density."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide drill-down or a linked table when readers need original rows."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a spatial index only when individual points remain the correct\nrepresentation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep keyboard navigation bounded to useful targets instead of stepping\nthrough every source observation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep exact values outside hover-only UI."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An index can accelerate nearest-point lookup. It does not reduce scene size,\nDOM or draw count, path construction, paint cost, or visual overplotting."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"streaming-windows","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming windows"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a rolling source:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"store complete history outside the renderer when the product needs it;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"pass a bounded window or envelope;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"preserve keys for surviving observations;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"decide whether the viewport follows the newest data;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"preserve a locked viewport during offscreen updates;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"announce meaningful offscreen changes through application UI."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Measure same-key value changes, partial rolls, full replacement, resize, and a\nsustained update stream. A fast mount does not prove a healthy update path."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"performance-measurement","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Performance measurement"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"deterministic source creation;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"analytical preparation;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"scene compilation;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SVG serialization;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DOM reconciliation;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Canvas painting;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"pointer activation and sustained movement;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"memory after repeated updates and destroy."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Validation hashes and invariant checks belong outside timed preparation.\nReport failed correctness cells as failures, not as fast results."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The repository's stress harness provides product-shaped and encoded workloads:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"pnpm benchmark:stress:quick\npnpm benchmark:stress:standard","lang":"bash"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use focused filters while developing, then run the canonical profile before a\nrelease. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./bundle-size-and-performance.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bundle Size and Performance"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor current budgets and import boundaries."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"large-data-checklist","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Large-data checklist"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The representation answers the stated question."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every source row is accounted for or sampling is disclosed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Totals, extrema, ordering, and identity invariants are executable."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source, represented, prepared, and rendered counts are reported separately."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Width-dependent preparation invalidates on resize."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Output complexity is bounded in its native units."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rolling data preserves surviving keys."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dense focus targets match the represented meaning."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exact values have a table, drill-down, or textual alternative."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"count-every-stage","text":"Count every stage","level":2},{"id":"bounded-representations","text":"Bounded representations","level":2},{"id":"when-raw-output-is-still-useful","text":"When raw output is still useful","level":2},{"id":"when-canvas-helps","text":"When Canvas helps","level":2},{"id":"transform-ownership","text":"Transform ownership","level":2},{"id":"interaction-over-dense-output","text":"Interaction over dense output","level":2},{"id":"streaming-windows","text":"Streaming windows","level":2},{"id":"performance-measurement","text":"Performance measurement","level":2},{"id":"large-data-checklist","text":"Large-data checklist","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nLarge-data charting starts with representation, not renderer throughput.\n\nAsk:\n\n1. What question must the chart answer?\n2. Must a reader inspect every observation?\n3. How many independently useful marks fit in the available pixels?\n4. Which totals, extrema, order, or identities must never be lost?\n5. How frequently does the source update?\n\nRaw output is correct only while independently rendering every observation is\nboth meaningful and affordable.\n\n## Count every stage\n\nTrack four quantities:\n\n- **source**: rows received by the application;\n- **represented**: source rows accounted for by the encoding;\n- **prepared**: rows or vertices passed to marks;\n- **rendered**: scene nodes and path vertices, plus the selected renderer's DOM\n  or draw work.\n\nDo not describe a million-row chart as a million rendered points when the\nvisible result is a few thousand aggregate cells. The bounded output is a\nfeature, but its accounting must be honest.\n\n## Bounded representations\n\n| Problem                          | Representation                           | Invariant                                                  |\n| -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Dense point cloud                | Fixed density grid                       | Cell counts sum to source count                            |\n| High-rate ordered signal         | Pixel-width first/min/max/last envelope  | Every row belongs to one bucket and global extrema survive |\n| Numeric distribution             | Fixed-boundary histogram                 | Bin counts sum to source count                             |\n| High-cardinality categories      | Leading categories plus Other            | Leading and remainder totals sum to source count           |\n| Large time range with a viewport | Domain-clipped or width-bounded envelope | Visible extrema and boundary continuity survive            |\n\nChoose bucket count from the task and available resolution. A test fixture's\ngrid or threshold count is not a universal default.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=73-many-point-scatter height=480 -->\n\n## When raw output is still useful\n\n- One line path can retain many ordered observations without creating one DOM\n  node per row.\n- A scatterplot creates an independently addressable mark for every\n  observation.\n- Stable keys make rolling updates reuse surviving nodes.\n\nIdentity reuse reduces allocation and replacement. It cannot make ten thousand\nindependent SVG elements a sensible default when those elements compete for\nthe same pixels.\n\nSwitch representations before the raw update cost exceeds the product's frame\nbudget or the output stops being readable.\n\n## When Canvas helps\n\nThe optional Canvas renderer removes per-node SVG DOM and reconciliation cost.\nIt can be useful when a measured bottleneck is SVG element creation or paint.\nIt does not remove channel materialization, scale and guide work, scene\ncompilation, path construction, or `ChartPoint` creation.\n\nA dot mark still produces one scene node and one interaction point per\nobservation. Default nearest-point focus still scans those points linearly.\nSupply a measured `spatialIndex` when individual points are still the correct\npointer target, and use a focus strategy with a bounded `navigation` order\nwhen every observation is not a useful keyboard stop.\n\nDo not treat Canvas as permission to promise a million independently\ninteractive marks. Compare source, represented, prepared, and rendered counts;\nmeasure compilation, paint, interaction, and memory; then aggregate, sample,\nor bound the window when the representation exceeds the product budget.\n\n## Transform ownership\n\nPut width-independent aggregation in application code. Memoize it with the\nframework's computed-state primitive when it should survive a presentation\nchange.\n\nSurface-responsive transforms may use the responsive builder's full scene width.\nExact screen-space transforms need the final inner plot bounds and resolved\nscales, so implement them in a custom mark's render phase or an\napplication-owned overlay. Keep either cost visible rather than hiding it in\nan unrelated renderer.\n\n```ts\nfunction summarizeDensity(rows: Input['rows']) {\n  return summarizeSource(rows)\n}\n\nfunction createDensityChart(rows: Input['rows']) {\n  const summary = summarizeDensity(rows)\n\n  return defineChart(({ width }) => {\n    const thresholds = width < 480 ? 12 : width < 900 ? 24 : 40\n    const bins = buildSemanticBins(summary, { thresholds })\n    return densitySpec(bins)\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe full scene dimensions equal final plot dimensions only for a deliberately\nguide-free, zero-margin chart. See\n[Responsive Charts](./responsive-charts.md#responsive-construction) for the\ngeometry boundary.\n\nUse the official D3 modules linked from\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) for grouping, binning, summary\nstatistics, spatial indexing, and shape preparation. TanStack Charts consumes\nthe resulting rows and intervals.\n\n## Interaction over dense output\n\nFirst decide whether the encoded mark or the original observation is the focus\ntarget.\n\n- Focus aggregate cells when the chart communicates density.\n- Provide drill-down or a linked table when readers need original rows.\n- Use a spatial index only when individual points remain the correct\n  representation.\n- Keep keyboard navigation bounded to useful targets instead of stepping\n  through every source observation.\n- Keep exact values outside hover-only UI.\n\nAn index can accelerate nearest-point lookup. It does not reduce scene size,\nDOM or draw count, path construction, paint cost, or visual overplotting.\n\n## Streaming windows\n\nFor a rolling source:\n\n- store complete history outside the renderer when the product needs it;\n- pass a bounded window or envelope;\n- preserve keys for surviving observations;\n- decide whether the viewport follows the newest data;\n- preserve a locked viewport during offscreen updates;\n- announce meaningful offscreen changes through application UI.\n\nMeasure same-key value changes, partial rolls, full replacement, resize, and a\nsustained update stream. A fast mount does not prove a healthy update path.\n\n## Performance measurement\n\nSeparate:\n\n- deterministic source creation;\n- analytical preparation;\n- scene compilation;\n- SVG serialization;\n- DOM reconciliation;\n- Canvas painting;\n- pointer activation and sustained movement;\n- memory after repeated updates and destroy.\n\nValidation hashes and invariant checks belong outside timed preparation.\nReport failed correctness cells as failures, not as fast results.\n\nThe repository's stress harness provides product-shaped and encoded workloads:\n\n```bash\npnpm benchmark:stress:quick\npnpm benchmark:stress:standard\n```\n\nUse focused filters while developing, then run the canonical profile before a\nrelease. See [Bundle Size and Performance](./bundle-size-and-performance.md)\nfor current budgets and import boundaries.\n\n## Large-data checklist\n\n- The representation answers the stated question.\n- Every source row is accounted for or sampling is disclosed.\n- Totals, extrema, ordering, and identity invariants are executable.\n- Source, represented, prepared, and rendered counts are reported separately.\n- Width-dependent preparation invalidates on resize.\n- Output complexity is bounded in its native units.\n- Rolling data preserves surviving keys.\n- Dense focus targets match the represented meaning.\n- Exact values have a table, drill-down, or textual alternative.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Large Data\ndescription: Choose bounded visual representations, measure preparation separately, preserve analytical invariants, and avoid rendering more detail than pixels can communicate.\n---\n\nLarge-data charting starts with representation, not renderer throughput.\n\nAsk:\n\n1. What question must the chart answer?\n2. Must a reader inspect every observation?\n3. How many independently useful marks fit in the available pixels?\n4. Which totals, extrema, order, or identities must never be lost?\n5. How frequently does the source update?\n\nRaw output is correct only while independently rendering every observation is\nboth meaningful and affordable.\n\n## Count every stage\n\nTrack four quantities:\n\n- **source**: rows received by the application;\n- **represented**: source rows accounted for by the encoding;\n- **prepared**: rows or vertices passed to marks;\n- **rendered**: scene nodes and path vertices, plus the selected renderer's DOM\n  or draw work.\n\nDo not describe a million-row chart as a million rendered points when the\nvisible result is a few thousand aggregate cells. The bounded output is a\nfeature, but its accounting must be honest.\n\n## Bounded representations\n\n| Problem                          | Representation                           | Invariant                                                  |\n| -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Dense point cloud                | Fixed density grid                       | Cell counts sum to source count                            |\n| High-rate ordered signal         | Pixel-width first/min/max/last envelope  | Every row belongs to one bucket and global extrema survive |\n| Numeric distribution             | Fixed-boundary histogram                 | Bin counts sum to source count                             |\n| High-cardinality categories      | Leading categories plus Other            | Leading and remainder totals sum to source count           |\n| Large time range with a viewport | Domain-clipped or width-bounded envelope | Visible extrema and boundary continuity survive            |\n\nChoose bucket count from the task and available resolution. A test fixture's\ngrid or threshold count is not a universal default.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=73-many-point-scatter height=480 -->\n\n## When raw output is still useful\n\n- One line path can retain many ordered observations without creating one DOM\n  node per row.\n- A scatterplot creates an independently addressable mark for every\n  observation.\n- Stable keys make rolling updates reuse surviving nodes.\n\nIdentity reuse reduces allocation and replacement. It cannot make ten thousand\nindependent SVG elements a sensible default when those elements compete for\nthe same pixels.\n\nSwitch representations before the raw update cost exceeds the product's frame\nbudget or the output stops being readable.\n\n## When Canvas helps\n\nThe optional Canvas renderer removes per-node SVG DOM and reconciliation cost.\nIt can be useful when a measured bottleneck is SVG element creation or paint.\nIt does not remove channel materialization, scale and guide work, scene\ncompilation, path construction, or `ChartPoint` creation.\n\nA dot mark still produces one scene node and one interaction point per\nobservation. Default nearest-point focus still scans those points linearly.\nSupply a measured `spatialIndex` when individual points are still the correct\npointer target, and use a focus strategy with a bounded `navigation` order\nwhen every observation is not a useful keyboard stop.\n\nDo not treat Canvas as permission to promise a million independently\ninteractive marks. Compare source, represented, prepared, and rendered counts;\nmeasure compilation, paint, interaction, and memory; then aggregate, sample,\nor bound the window when the representation exceeds the product budget.\n\n## Transform ownership\n\nPut width-independent aggregation in application code. Memoize it with the\nframework's computed-state primitive when it should survive a presentation\nchange.\n\nSurface-responsive transforms may use the responsive builder's full scene width.\nExact screen-space transforms need the final inner plot bounds and resolved\nscales, so implement them in a custom mark's render phase or an\napplication-owned overlay. Keep either cost visible rather than hiding it in\nan unrelated renderer.\n\n```ts\nfunction summarizeDensity(rows: Input['rows']) {\n  return summarizeSource(rows)\n}\n\nfunction createDensityChart(rows: Input['rows']) {\n  const summary = summarizeDensity(rows)\n\n  return defineChart(({ width }) => {\n    const thresholds = width < 480 ? 12 : width < 900 ? 24 : 40\n    const bins = buildSemanticBins(summary, { thresholds })\n    return densitySpec(bins)\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe full scene dimensions equal final plot dimensions only for a deliberately\nguide-free, zero-margin chart. See\n[Responsive Charts](./responsive-charts.md#responsive-construction) for the\ngeometry boundary.\n\nUse the official D3 modules linked from\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) for grouping, binning, summary\nstatistics, spatial indexing, and shape preparation. TanStack Charts consumes\nthe resulting rows and intervals.\n\n## Interaction over dense output\n\nFirst decide whether the encoded mark or the original observation is the focus\ntarget.\n\n- Focus aggregate cells when the chart communicates density.\n- Provide drill-down or a linked table when readers need original rows.\n- Use a spatial index only when individual points remain the correct\n  representation.\n- Keep keyboard navigation bounded to useful targets instead of stepping\n  through every source observation.\n- Keep exact values outside hover-only UI.\n\nAn index can accelerate nearest-point lookup. It does not reduce scene size,\nDOM or draw count, path construction, paint cost, or visual overplotting.\n\n## Streaming windows\n\nFor a rolling source:\n\n- store complete history outside the renderer when the product needs it;\n- pass a bounded window or envelope;\n- preserve keys for surviving observations;\n- decide whether the viewport follows the newest data;\n- preserve a locked viewport during offscreen updates;\n- announce meaningful offscreen changes through application UI.\n\nMeasure same-key value changes, partial rolls, full replacement, resize, and a\nsustained update stream. A fast mount does not prove a healthy update path.\n\n## Performance measurement\n\nSeparate:\n\n- deterministic source creation;\n- analytical preparation;\n- scene compilation;\n- SVG serialization;\n- DOM reconciliation;\n- Canvas painting;\n- pointer activation and sustained movement;\n- memory after repeated updates and destroy.\n\nValidation hashes and invariant checks belong outside timed preparation.\nReport failed correctness cells as failures, not as fast results.\n\nThe repository's stress harness provides product-shaped and encoded workloads:\n\n```bash\npnpm benchmark:stress:quick\npnpm benchmark:stress:standard\n```\n\nUse focused filters while developing, then run the canonical profile before a\nrelease. See [Bundle Size and Performance](./bundle-size-and-performance.md)\nfor current budgets and import boundaries.\n\n## Large-data checklist\n\n- The representation answers the stated question.\n- Every source row is accounted for or sampling is disclosed.\n- Totals, extrema, ordering, and identity invariants are executable.\n- Source, represented, prepared, and rendered counts are reported separately.\n- Width-dependent preparation invalidates on resize.\n- Output complexity is bounded in its native units.\n- Rolling data preserves surviving keys.\n- Dense focus targets match the represented meaning.\n- Exact values have a table, drill-down, or textual alternative.\n"}
